response:
To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function or the `return` statement.

The `exit()` function is used to exit the program immediately and return control to the operating system. It takes an integer argument that specifies the status code of the termination:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, World!\n");
    exit(0); // Terminate the program with status code 0 (success)
}
```

In this example, when the program reaches the `exit(0)` line, it will print "Hello, World!" and then terminate immediately.

Alternatively, you can use the `return` statement to terminate the program after performing some operations. The value returned from the `return` statement becomes the status code of the termination:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int result = calculateSomething();
    if(result == -1) { // Assume calculateSomething() returns -1 on error
        return -1; // Return -1 as the status code
    }
    printf("Result: %d\n", result);
    return 0; // Return 0 as the status code
}

